<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yujiulü Chounu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 520</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yujiulü Chounu was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> of the Rouran from 508 to 520. He succeeded his father Futu, who had been killed by the Gaoche, and in 516 he defeated and killed the Gaoche king Mi'etu. He sent envoys to the Northern Wei, among them a Buddhist monk in 511. He was killed in 520 in a conflict at his court, and his brother Anagui succeeded him.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yujiulü Chounu, who bore the title Douluofubadoufa Khagan, was the eldest son of Khagan Yujiulü Futu. He succeeded when his father was killed in battle against the Gaoche, at a time when Qu Jia, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a>, had rebelled against the Rouran and submitted again to the Gaoche.</p><p>He sent the Buddhist monk Hong Xuan to the Northern Wei court, where the monk presented Emperor Xuanwu with a statue inlaid with pearls. Xuanwu decided to send General Ma Yishu as an envoy in return in 514, but the emperor died soon after and the embassy was cancelled; that same year Chounu turned instead to the Liang dynasty and sent it his envoy Qijin Bijian.</p><p>In 516 he marched against the Gaoche to avenge his father. The campaign succeeded: the Gaoche leader Mietu was captured and dragged to death by a horse. Chounu then sent envoys with gifts to both the Northern Wei and the Liang, and in 519 a tribute of 700 slaves went to the Wei court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Chounu's last years were overshadowed by a palace intrigue. When his infant brother Zuhui disappeared, a twenty-year-old shamaness, Dauhun Diwan, announced that spirits had taken the child alive to heaven and could return him. After a seven-day prayer service Zuhui ran into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>'s yurt, saying he had lived in heaven for a year. Chounu made Diwan divorce her husband Fusheng, who was given 3,000 horses and bulls, married her, and ceased to attend to the affairs of state.</p><p>When Zuhui grew up, the dowager khatun Hou Luling found that he had in fact been kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a>'s yurt. Chounu refused to believe it and, persuaded by Diwan, had Zuhui executed, whereupon the dowager had her servants strangle Diwan. At the same time the Gaoche attacked and defeated the Rouran. Chounu returned to his headquarters to punish his wife's killers, but on his mother's orders the princes put him to death and raised his younger brother Anagui to the throne in 520.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yujiulü Chounu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion combined steppe cults of Heaven with shamanic practices, and Buddhism also reached the court: in 511 the khagan <b>Chounu</b> sent a monk as his envoy to the Northern Wei.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
